WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.734
In a galvanic cell, the electrons flow by themselves. In an electrolytic cell, a power supply pushes them the other way. It forces a reaction that would not happen on its own.

00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:22.062
The electrode joined to the negative terminal is the cathode, where reduction happens. The electrode joined to the positive terminal is the anode, where oxidation happens.

00:00:22.662 --> 00:00:31.865
So in an electrolytic cell, the anode is positive. The signs change, but the jobs never do. Oxidation is always at the anode.

00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:36.351
Electrolysis is two contests.

00:00:36.951 --> 00:00:42.233
At the cathode, every oxidising agent present competes, and the strongest one is reduced.

00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:47.974
At the anode, every reducing agent present competes, and the strongest one is oxidised.

00:00:48.574 --> 00:00:52.962
In a solution, there is always one extra player in both contests: water.

00:00:55.462 --> 00:01:02.415
At the cathode, water can be reduced to hydrogen gas and hydroxide ions, at minus zero comma eight three volts.

00:01:03.015 --> 00:01:09.633
At the anode, water can be oxidised to oxygen gas and hydrogen ions, at plus one comma two three volts.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:14.088
Compare each ion with water. The stronger one wins.

00:01:16.588 --> 00:01:24.411
Dilute sodium chloride solution is electrolysed with graphite electrodes. What forms at the cathode? Decide now.

00:01:29.011 --> 00:01:40.909
Hydrogen gas. Sodium ions, at minus two comma seven one volts, are a much weaker oxidising agent than water. So water is reduced, and hydrogen bubbles off.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:59.239
Sodium ions are attracted to the cathode, but that does not mean they react. Attraction decides only which way the ions drift. Table 4 decides which species reacts. Sodium forms only from molten sodium chloride, where there is no water.

00:02:01.739 --> 00:02:14.744
At the anode, water, at plus one comma two three volts, is a slightly stronger reducing agent than chloride, at plus one comma three six volts. So in dilute sodium chloride, oxygen forms.

00:02:15.344 --> 00:02:27.580
But in a concentrated chloride solution, the high concentration of chloride ions makes the oxidation of chloride predominant. Chlorine gas forms. That is why brine is used to make chlorine.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:46.938
An anode that is not inert is in the anode contest too. Copper, at plus zero comma three four volts, is a stronger reducing agent than water. So a copper anode is oxidised itself, and dissolves. Platinum and graphite are the usual inert electrodes.

00:02:49.438 --> 00:02:57.809
Open the electrolysis lab in Predict mode. Choose an electrolyte, predict both products, then run it and read the two contests.
